    interviewType:     "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription:     "When an Activity undergoes a runtime configuration change (such as device screen rotation, theme switching between dark/light mode, locale change, or multi-window resizing), Android destroys the current Activity instance",
    explanation:     "When an Activity undergoes a runtime configuration change (such as device screen rotation, theme switching between dark/light mode, locale change, or multi-window resizing), Android destroys the current Activity instance",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nWhen an Activity undergoes a runtime configuration change (such as device screen rotation, theme switching between dark/light mode, locale change, or multi-window resizing), Android destroys the current Activity instance (calling onPause, onStop, onDestroy) and instantiates a brand new one. Any state stored exclusively in View properties or Activity instance fields is lost.\n\nTo retain state effectively, Android provides three complementary tiers:\n1. ViewModel: Survives configuration changes because its lifecycle is scoped to the `ViewModelStoreOwner` (which retains the ViewModel across Activity recreate events until the Activity is permanently finished). Ideal for UI state, cached data, and active business operations.\n2. SavedStateHandle / onSaveInstanceState: Survives both configuration changes and OS-initiated System-Initiated Process Death (when the OS kills the process in the background to reclaim RAM). It serializes small data (< 500KB - 1MB transaction buffer limit) into a `Bundle`.\n3. Persistent Storage (Room / DataStore / Preferences): Survives app restarts, process death, and full device reboots.\n\nFor retaining an in-memory selection (like a selected sector/tab/item):\n- Store the state in a `StateFlow` inside a `ViewModel`.\n- Injected with `SavedStateHandle` so that if Android kills the background process due to low memory, the selected item key is restored seamlessly when the user returns.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nWhen an Activity undergoes a runtime configuration change (such as device screen rotation, theme switching between dark/light mode, locale change, or multi-window resizing), Android destroys the current Activity instance (calling onPause, onStop, onDestroy) and instantiates a brand new one. Any state stored exclusively in View properties or Activity instance fields is lost.\n\nTo retain state effectively, Android provides three complementary tiers:\n1. ViewModel: Survives configuration changes because its lifecycle is scoped to the `ViewModelStoreOwner` (which retains the ViewModel across Activity recreate events until the Activity is permanently finished). Ideal for UI state, cached data, and active business operations.\n2. SavedStateHandle / onSaveInstanceState: Survives both configuration changes and OS-initiated System-Initiated Process Death (when the OS kills the process in the background to reclaim RAM). It serializes small data (< 500KB - 1MB transaction buffer limit) into a `Bundle`.\n3. Persistent Storage (Room / DataStore / Preferences): Survives app restarts, process death, and full device reboots.\n\nFor retaining an in-memory selection (like a selected sector/tab/item):\n- Store the state in a `StateFlow` inside a `ViewModel`.\n- Injected with `SavedStateHandle` so that if Android kills the background process due to low memory, the selected item key is restored seamlessly when the user returns.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// 1. ViewModel managing state backed by SavedStateHandle (survives both config change & process death)\nclass SectorSelectionViewModel(\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    companion object {\n        private const val KEY_SELECTED_SECTOR = \"key_selected_sector\"\n    }\n\n    // SavedStateHandle provides a StateFlow that automatically persists to and restores from Bundle\n    val selectedSector: StateFlow<String?> = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(KEY_SELECTED_SECTOR, null)\n\n    fun selectSector(sectorId: String) {\n        savedStateHandle[KEY_SELECTED_SECTOR] = sectorId\n    }\n}\n\n// 2. Activity observing the StateFlow safely across lifecycle states\nclass SectorActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n\n    private val viewModel: SectorSelectionViewModel by viewModels()\n    private lateinit var binding: ActivitySectorBinding\n\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n        binding = ActivitySectorBinding.inflate(layoutInflater)\n        setContentView(binding.root)\n\n        setupSectorButtons()\n        observeState()\n    }\n\n    private fun setupSectorButtons() {\n        binding.btnFintech.setOnClickListener { viewModel.selectSector(\"FINTECH\") }\n        binding.btnHealthCare.setOnClickListener { viewModel.selectSector(\"HEALTHCARE\") }\n    }\n\n    private fun observeState() {\n        lifecycleScope.launch {\n            repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n                viewModel.selectedSector.collect { sector ->\n                    binding.tvSelectedStatus.text = \"Selected: ${sector ?: \"None\"}\"\n                    highlightSelectedButton(sector)\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun highlightSelectedButton(sector: String?) {\n        binding.btnFintech.isSelected = (sector == \"FINTECH\")\n        binding.btnHealthCare.isSelected = (sector == \"HEALTHCARE\")\n    }\n}\n```",
