    explanation:     "A Fragment has two intertwined lifecycles:\n1. Fragment Lifecycle: Tracks the Fragment object itself (from `onAttach`, `onCreate`, down to `onDestroy`, `onDetach`).\n2. Fragment View Lifecycle: Tracks the active View hiera",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nA Fragment has two intertwined lifecycles:\n1. Fragment Lifecycle: Tracks the Fragment object itself (from `onAttach`, `onCreate`, down to `onDestroy`, `onDetach`).\n2. Fragment View Lifecycle: Tracks the active View hierarchy created in `onCreateView` and destroyed in `onDestroyView`.\n\nCrucial architectural distinction:\nWhen a Fragment transaction is replaced and added to the back stack (`addToBackStack(null)`), the Fragment instance remains in memory on the back stack, but its View is completely destroyed. Therefore:\n- Fragment enters: `onDestroyView()`, but NOT `onDestroy()`.\n- When popped back from back stack: `onCreateView()` and `onViewCreated()` are called again, but `onCreate()` is NOT called again.\n- Memory Leak Risk: Any reference to Views or ViewBinding held in the Fragment instance after `onDestroyView()` causes a severe memory leak. ViewBinding must be nulled out in `onDestroyView()`.\n- Coroutine / Observer Scope: Observers must ALWAYS observe using `viewLifecycleOwner` inside `onViewCreated()`, NEVER `this` (the Fragment instance). Observing with `this` leads to duplicate observers every time the Fragment returns from the back stack.\n\nNavigation Scenarios:\n- Fragment A replaces Fragment B (without Back Stack): A -> onAttach..onResume. B -> onPause..onDestroy..onDetach.\n- Fragment A replaces Fragment B (WITH Back Stack): B -> onPause, onStop, onDestroyView (B stays alive in memory). A -> onAttach..onResume.\n- User presses Back (Pop back stack): A -> onPause, onStop, onDestroyView, onDestroy, onDetach. B -> onCreateView, onViewCreated, onStart, onResume.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nA Fragment has two intertwined lifecycles:\n1. Fragment Lifecycle: Tracks the Fragment object itself (from `onAttach`, `onCreate`, down to `onDestroy`, `onDetach`).\n2. Fragment View Lifecycle: Tracks the active View hierarchy created in `onCreateView` and destroyed in `onDestroyView`.\n\nCrucial architectural distinction:\nWhen a Fragment transaction is replaced and added to the back stack (`addToBackStack(null)`), the Fragment instance remains in memory on the back stack, but its View is completely destroyed. Therefore:\n- Fragment enters: `onDestroyView()`, but NOT `onDestroy()`.\n- When popped back from back stack: `onCreateView()` and `onViewCreated()` are called again, but `onCreate()` is NOT called again.\n- Memory Leak Risk: Any reference to Views or ViewBinding held in the Fragment instance after `onDestroyView()` causes a severe memory leak. ViewBinding must be nulled out in `onDestroyView()`.\n- Coroutine / Observer Scope: Observers must ALWAYS observe using `viewLifecycleOwner` inside `onViewCreated()`, NEVER `this` (the Fragment instance). Observing with `this` leads to duplicate observers every time the Fragment returns from the back stack.\n\nNavigation Scenarios:\n- Fragment A replaces Fragment B (without Back Stack): A -> onAttach..onResume. B -> onPause..onDestroy..onDetach.\n- Fragment A replaces Fragment B (WITH Back Stack): B -> onPause, onStop, onDestroyView (B stays alive in memory). A -> onAttach..onResume.\n- User presses Back (Pop back stack): A -> onPause, onStop, onDestroyView, onDestroy, onDetach. B -> onCreateView, onViewCreated, onStart, onResume.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\nclass ProductDetailsFragment : Fragment(R.layout.fragment_product_details) {\n\n    // ViewBinding backing property idiom to prevent memory leaks\n    private var _binding: FragmentProductDetailsBinding? = null\n    private val binding get() = _binding!!\n\n    private val viewModel: ProductDetailsViewModel by viewModels()\n\n    override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)\n        _binding = FragmentProductDetailsBinding.bind(view)\n\n        // ALWAYS use viewLifecycleOwner for UI observations\n        viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {\n            viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n                viewModel.uiState.collect { state ->\n                    render(state)\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun render(state: ProductUiState) {\n        binding.tvTitle.text = state.title\n        binding.tvPrice.text = state.priceFormatted\n        binding.progressBar.isVisible = state.isLoading\n    }\n\n    // MANDATORY: Null out view binding to avoid leaking View hierarchy on back stack\n    override fun onDestroyView() {\n        super.onDestroyView()\n        _binding = null\n    }\n}\n```",
