    shortDescription:     "1. Kotlin Fundamentals for Android:\n- Null Safety: Non-nullable (`String`) vs Nullable (`String?`), Safe call (`?.`), Elvis operator (`?:`), and Safe Cast (`as?`). Eliminates `NullPointerException` at compile time.\n- Dat",
    explanation:     "1. Kotlin Fundamentals for Android:\n- Null Safety: Non-nullable (`String`) vs Nullable (`String?`), Safe call (`?.`), Elvis operator (`?:`), and Safe Cast (`as?`). Eliminates `NullPointerException` at compile time.\n- Dat",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\n1. Kotlin Fundamentals for Android:\n- Null Safety: Non-nullable (`String`) vs Nullable (`String?`), Safe call (`?.`), Elvis operator (`?:`), and Safe Cast (`as?`). Eliminates `NullPointerException` at compile time.\n- Data Classes: Automatically generates `equals()`, `hashCode()`, `toString()`, `copy()`, and component functions. Perfect for immutability and MVI/MVVM UI states.\n- Sealed Classes & Sealed Interfaces: Restricted class hierarchies where all subclasses are known at compile time. Essential for modeling exhaustive UI states (Loading, Success, Error) with `when` statements without requiring an `else` branch.\n- Extension Functions: Enables extending third-party or platform classes (e.g., `Context`, `View`) without inheritance.\n- Coroutines: Lightweight cooperative multitasking threads for asynchronous, non-blocking I/O.\n- Scope Functions: `let`, `apply`, `run`, `also`, `with` for cleaner object initialization and transformations.\n\n2. Android Core Components:\n- Activity: Single focused UI window for user interaction.\n- Service: Background execution component without UI (Foreground Service with persistent notification, or background work via WorkManager).\n- BroadcastReceiver: Inter-app and system-wide asynchronous event receiver (e.g., battery low, airplane mode, custom app broadcasts).\n- ContentProvider: Manages structured access to a shared relational repository of data across application boundaries (e.g., Contacts, MediaStore).\n\nModern Android also relies on Jetpack Architecture Components (ViewModel, Room, Navigation, Jetpack Compose) to build reactive, lifecycle-safe architectures.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\n1. Kotlin Fundamentals for Android:\n- Null Safety: Non-nullable (`String`) vs Nullable (`String?`), Safe call (`?.`), Elvis operator (`?:`), and Safe Cast (`as?`). Eliminates `NullPointerException` at compile time.\n- Data Classes: Automatically generates `equals()`, `hashCode()`, `toString()`, `copy()`, and component functions. Perfect for immutability and MVI/MVVM UI states.\n- Sealed Classes & Sealed Interfaces: Restricted class hierarchies where all subclasses are known at compile time. Essential for modeling exhaustive UI states (Loading, Success, Error) with `when` statements without requiring an `else` branch.\n- Extension Functions: Enables extending third-party or platform classes (e.g., `Context`, `View`) without inheritance.\n- Coroutines: Lightweight cooperative multitasking threads for asynchronous, non-blocking I/O.\n- Scope Functions: `let`, `apply`, `run`, `also`, `with` for cleaner object initialization and transformations.\n\n2. Android Core Components:\n- Activity: Single focused UI window for user interaction.\n- Service: Background execution component without UI (Foreground Service with persistent notification, or background work via WorkManager).\n- BroadcastReceiver: Inter-app and system-wide asynchronous event receiver (e.g., battery low, airplane mode, custom app broadcasts).\n- ContentProvider: Manages structured access to a shared relational repository of data across application boundaries (e.g., Contacts, MediaStore).\n\nModern Android also relies on Jetpack Architecture Components (ViewModel, Room, Navigation, Jetpack Compose) to build reactive, lifecycle-safe architectures.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// 1. Sealed Interface modeling complete UI State exhaustively\nsealed interface UserUiState {\n    object Loading : UserUiState\n    data class Success(val user: UserProfile) : UserUiState\n    data class Error(val message: String) : UserUiState\n}\n\n// 2. Immutable Data Class\ndata class UserProfile(\n    val id: String,\n    val name: String,\n    val email: String? // Nullable field\n)\n\n// 3. Extension function utilizing Elvis operator and Safe calls\nfun Context.showToast(message: String, duration: Int = Toast.LENGTH_SHORT) {\n    Toast.makeText(this, message, duration).show()\n}\n\n// 4. Activity utilizing Kotlin features to render state exhaustively\nclass UserProfileActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n\n    fun render(state: UserUiState) {\n        // Compiler enforces handling all sealed subclasses without needing an 'else' branch\n        when (state) {\n            is UserUiState.Loading -> {\n                showLoading(true)\n            }\n            is UserUiState.Success -> {\n                showLoading(false)\n                val displayName = state.user.name.ifBlank { \"Anonymous\" }\n                val contactEmail = state.user.email ?: \"No email provided\"\n                showToast(\"Welcome $displayName ($contactEmail)\")\n            }\n            is UserUiState.Error -> {\n                showLoading(false)\n                showToast(\"Error: ${state.message}\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun showLoading(active: Boolean) { /* update UI */ }\n}\n```",
