    shortDescription:     "The `suspend` keyword indicates that a function can pause its execution and later resume without blocking the thread it is executing on. Under the Hood (Continuation Passing Style & State Machine):\n1. Continuation Passin",
    explanation:     "The `suspend` keyword indicates that a function can pause its execution and later resume without blocking the thread it is executing on. Under the Hood (Continuation Passing Style & State Machine):\n1. Continuation Passin",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nThe `suspend` keyword indicates that a function can pause its execution and later resume without blocking the thread it is executing on.\n\nUnder the Hood (Continuation Passing Style & State Machine):\n1. Continuation Passing Style (CPS): At compile time, the Kotlin compiler transforms every `suspend` function by adding an extra hidden parameter: `Continuation<T>`.\n   - `suspend fun fetchData(): String` becomes `fun fetchData(continuation: Continuation<String>): Any?`\n2. State Machine: The compiler decomposes the function body into a state machine. Each suspension point (e.g., `delay()`, network call, database read) represents a distinct state (label).\n3. Suspension: When a suspend function reaches a suspension point that cannot complete immediately, it returns the sentinel object `COROUTINE_SUSPENDED`. The underlying OS thread is completely freed to execute other tasks (e.g., rendering UI frames on the Main thread).\n4. Resumption: When the background I/O or timer completes, the framework calls `continuation.resumeWith(result)`. The state machine resumes right where it left off, on whichever thread dispatcher is configured.\n\nCrucial distinction: `suspend` does NOT automatically mean background thread. Calling a suspend function that performs a heavy computation on `Dispatchers.Main` without `withContext(Dispatchers.Default)` WILL freeze the Main thread and cause an ANR.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nThe `suspend` keyword indicates that a function can pause its execution and later resume without blocking the thread it is executing on.\n\nUnder the Hood (Continuation Passing Style & State Machine):\n1. Continuation Passing Style (CPS): At compile time, the Kotlin compiler transforms every `suspend` function by adding an extra hidden parameter: `Continuation<T>`.\n   - `suspend fun fetchData(): String` becomes `fun fetchData(continuation: Continuation<String>): Any?`\n2. State Machine: The compiler decomposes the function body into a state machine. Each suspension point (e.g., `delay()`, network call, database read) represents a distinct state (label).\n3. Suspension: When a suspend function reaches a suspension point that cannot complete immediately, it returns the sentinel object `COROUTINE_SUSPENDED`. The underlying OS thread is completely freed to execute other tasks (e.g., rendering UI frames on the Main thread).\n4. Resumption: When the background I/O or timer completes, the framework calls `continuation.resumeWith(result)`. The state machine resumes right where it left off, on whichever thread dispatcher is configured.\n\nCrucial distinction: `suspend` does NOT automatically mean background thread. Calling a suspend function that performs a heavy computation on `Dispatchers.Main` without `withContext(Dispatchers.Default)` WILL freeze the Main thread and cause an ANR.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\nclass ProfileRepository(\n    private val apiService: UserApiService,\n    private val ioDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.IO\n) {\n\n    // suspend function that switches context safely using withContext\n    suspend fun fetchUserProfile(userId: String): Result<UserProfile> {\n        // withContext suspends the caller and switches execution to a thread from the IO pool\n        return withContext(ioDispatcher) {\n            try {\n                val response = apiService.getUser(userId) // Network call suspends here\n                Result.success(response.toDomain())\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                Result.failure(e)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// In ViewModel:\nclass ProfileViewModel(private val repository: ProfileRepository) : ViewModel() {\n\n    fun loadProfile() {\n        // viewModelScope runs on Dispatchers.Main.immediate by default\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            // UI thread is NOT blocked while fetchUserProfile waits for network\n            val result = repository.fetchUserProfile(\"123\")\n            result.onSuccess { user ->\n                // Directly update UI on the Main thread\n                updateUiWithUser(user)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun updateUiWithUser(user: UserProfile) { /* update UI */ }\n}\n```",
