    shortDescription:     "Jetpack Compose is a declarative UI toolkit where UI is a pure function of State (`UI = f(State)`). Composables can recompose frequently and unpredictably across any thread. Because of this, coroutines within Compose mus",
    explanation:     "Jetpack Compose is a declarative UI toolkit where UI is a pure function of State (`UI = f(State)`). Composables can recompose frequently and unpredictably across any thread. Because of this, coroutines within Compose mus",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is a declarative UI toolkit where UI is a pure function of State (`UI = f(State)`). Composables can recompose frequently and unpredictably across any thread. Because of this, coroutines within Compose must be strictly lifecycle-governed and side-effect controlled.\n\nCore Principles:\n1. Composables must be side-effect free: Launching coroutines directly inside the body of a Composable is a critical bug because recomposition will trigger duplicate, runaway coroutine executions.\n2. Effect Handlers for Coroutines:\n   - `LaunchedEffect(key)`: Launches a coroutine scoped to the Composable's lifecycle. Runs when entering composition; cancels and relaunches whenever `key` changes; cancels when leaving composition.\n   - `rememberCoroutineScope()`: Creates a coroutine scope bound to the composition lifecycle, used specifically inside event callbacks (like `onClick`) where composable functions cannot be called directly.\n3. Separation of Concerns:\n   - Long-running business operations, network calls, and data caching belong in `viewModelScope`.\n   - UI-only animations (like scrolling a list to top or showing a snackbar) belong in Compose coroutine scopes (`rememberCoroutineScope` or `LaunchedEffect`).",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is a declarative UI toolkit where UI is a pure function of State (`UI = f(State)`). Composables can recompose frequently and unpredictably across any thread. Because of this, coroutines within Compose must be strictly lifecycle-governed and side-effect controlled.\n\nCore Principles:\n1. Composables must be side-effect free: Launching coroutines directly inside the body of a Composable is a critical bug because recomposition will trigger duplicate, runaway coroutine executions.\n2. Effect Handlers for Coroutines:\n   - `LaunchedEffect(key)`: Launches a coroutine scoped to the Composable's lifecycle. Runs when entering composition; cancels and relaunches whenever `key` changes; cancels when leaving composition.\n   - `rememberCoroutineScope()`: Creates a coroutine scope bound to the composition lifecycle, used specifically inside event callbacks (like `onClick`) where composable functions cannot be called directly.\n3. Separation of Concerns:\n   - Long-running business operations, network calls, and data caching belong in `viewModelScope`.\n   - UI-only animations (like scrolling a list to top or showing a snackbar) belong in Compose coroutine scopes (`rememberCoroutineScope` or `LaunchedEffect`).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun UserSearchScreen(\n    query: String,\n    viewModel: UserSearchViewModel = viewModel()\n) {\n    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    val snackbarHostState = remember { SnackbarHostState() }\n    val scope = rememberCoroutineScope() // Bound to composition lifecycle for UI events\n\n    // 1. LaunchedEffect: Relaunches debounced search whenever 'query' changes\n    LaunchedEffect(query) {\n        viewModel.onQueryChanged(query)\n    }\n\n    // 2. LaunchedEffect for single-shot UI events (e.g. Error snackbar)\n    LaunchedEffect(uiState.errorMessage) {\n        uiState.errorMessage?.let { errorText ->\n            snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(errorText)\n            viewModel.dismissError()\n        }\n    }\n\n    Scaffold(\n        snackbarHost = { SnackbarHost(snackbarHostState) }\n    ) { paddingValues ->\n        Box(modifier = Modifier.padding(paddingValues)) {\n            // UI rendering based on state\n            if (uiState.isLoading) {\n                CircularProgressIndicator()\n            } else {\n                UserList(\n                    users = uiState.users,\n                    onItemClick = { userId ->\n                        // 3. rememberCoroutineScope used inside non-composable callback\n                        scope.launch {\n                            snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(\"Clicked user: $userId\")\n                        }\n                    }\n                )\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
