    question:     "Given coroutine code using launch, delay, and Thread.sleep, what output will it produce and in what order?",
    slug:     "android-11-given-coroutine-code-using-launch-delay-and-thread-sleep-what-output-will-it-produce-and-in-what-ord",
    categoryName:     "Android",
    categorySlug:     "android",
    subcategoryName:     "Android Development",
    subcategorySlug:     "android-development",
    experienceLevel:     "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty:     "MEDIUM",
    interviewType:     "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription:     "To determine execution order, apply the fundamental rules of coroutine scheduling:\n1. `launch` schedules a new coroutine on the designated dispatcher. It does NOT immediately execute inline unless using an unconfined or ",
    explanation:     "To determine execution order, apply the fundamental rules of coroutine scheduling:\n1. `launch` schedules a new coroutine on the designated dispatcher. It does NOT immediately execute inline unless using an unconfined or ",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nTo determine execution order, apply the fundamental rules of coroutine scheduling:\n1. `launch` schedules a new coroutine on the designated dispatcher. It does NOT immediately execute inline unless using an unconfined or immediate dispatcher; on standard dispatchers, it yields execution to let the calling block proceed until the next suspension or thread release point.\n2. `delay()` suspends the current coroutine without blocking the underlying thread, releasing the thread to run other scheduled coroutines.\n3. `Thread.sleep()` blocks the entire operating system thread. No other coroutine assigned to that thread can execute while it is sleeping.\n4. `runBlocking` blocks the calling thread until all coroutines launched within its scope have completed.\n\nCode Analysis Scenario:\n```kotlin\nfun main() = runBlocking {\n    launch {\n        delay(100)\n        println(\"A\")\n    }\n    launch {\n        Thread.sleep(50)\n        println(\"B\")\n    }\n    println(\"C\")\n}\n```\n\nStep-by-Step Execution Trace:\n1. `runBlocking` starts executing on the Main thread.\n2. `launch { delay(100); println(\"A\") }` schedules Coroutine 1 into the `runBlocking` event loop queue.\n3. `launch { Thread.sleep(50); println(\"B\") }` schedules Coroutine 2 into the event loop queue.\n4. The main thread continues running sequentially and reaches `println(\"C\")`. Therefore, **C** is printed first!\n5. The main block finishes its initial pass and checks the event loop queue. It picks up Coroutine 1.\n6. Coroutine 1 calls `delay(100)`. It suspends and schedules its resumption in 100ms, immediately freeing the Main thread!\n7. The Main thread immediately picks up Coroutine 2 from the queue.\n8. Coroutine 2 calls `Thread.sleep(50)`. It blocks the Main thread for 50ms.\n9. After 50ms, Coroutine 2 wakes up, executes `println(\"B\")`. Therefore, **B** is printed second!\n10. Coroutine 2 completes. Meanwhile, 50ms have elapsed. The thread waits the remaining ~50ms for Coroutine 1's timer.\n11. Coroutine 1 resumes and executes `println(\"A\")`. Therefore, **A** is printed third!\n\nFinal Output Sequence:\n```\nC\nB\nA\n```",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nTo determine execution order, apply the fundamental rules of coroutine scheduling:\n1. `launch` schedules a new coroutine on the designated dispatcher. It does NOT immediately execute inline unless using an unconfined or immediate dispatcher; on standard dispatchers, it yields execution to let the calling block proceed until the next suspension or thread release point.\n2. `delay()` suspends the current coroutine without blocking the underlying thread, releasing the thread to run other scheduled coroutines.\n3. `Thread.sleep()` blocks the entire operating system thread. No other coroutine assigned to that thread can execute while it is sleeping.\n4. `runBlocking` blocks the calling thread until all coroutines launched within its scope have completed.\n\nCode Analysis Scenario:\n```kotlin\nfun main() = runBlocking {\n    launch {\n        delay(100)\n        println(\"A\")\n    }\n    launch {\n        Thread.sleep(50)\n        println(\"B\")\n    }\n    println(\"C\")\n}\n```\n\nStep-by-Step Execution Trace:\n1. `runBlocking` starts executing on the Main thread.\n2. `launch { delay(100); println(\"A\") }` schedules Coroutine 1 into the `runBlocking` event loop queue.\n3. `launch { Thread.sleep(50); println(\"B\") }` schedules Coroutine 2 into the event loop queue.\n4. The main thread continues running sequentially and reaches `println(\"C\")`. Therefore, **C** is printed first!\n5. The main block finishes its initial pass and checks the event loop queue. It picks up Coroutine 1.\n6. Coroutine 1 calls `delay(100)`. It suspends and schedules its resumption in 100ms, immediately freeing the Main thread!\n7. The Main thread immediately picks up Coroutine 2 from the queue.\n8. Coroutine 2 calls `Thread.sleep(50)`. It blocks the Main thread for 50ms.\n9. After 50ms, Coroutine 2 wakes up, executes `println(\"B\")`. Therefore, **B** is printed second!\n10. Coroutine 2 completes. Meanwhile, 50ms have elapsed. The thread waits the remaining ~50ms for Coroutine 1's timer.\n11. Coroutine 1 resumes and executes `println(\"A\")`. Therefore, **A** is printed third!\n\nFinal Output Sequence:\n```\nC\nB\nA\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\nimport kotlinx.coroutines.*\n\nfun main() = runBlocking {\n    println(\"Start\")\n\n    launch {\n        println(\"Coroutine 1 started\")\n        delay(100) // Suspends: releases thread\n        println(\"A\")\n    }\n\n    launch {\n        println(\"Coroutine 2 started\")\n        Thread.sleep(50) // Blocks thread: nothing else can run on this thread\n        println(\"B\")\n    }\n\n    println(\"C\")\n    println(\"End of main block\")\n}\n// Guaranteed Output:\n// Start\n// C\n// End of main block\n// Coroutine 1 started\n// Coroutine 2 started\n// B\n// A\n```",
