    explanation:     "Kotlin Coroutines provide structured asynchronous programming. Unlike unmanaged threads, coroutines are organized in a strict parent-child hierarchy governed by three pillars:\n1. `CoroutineScope`: Defines the lifecycle b",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nKotlin Coroutines provide structured asynchronous programming. Unlike unmanaged threads, coroutines are organized in a strict parent-child hierarchy governed by three pillars:\n1. `CoroutineScope`: Defines the lifecycle boundary of coroutines.\n2. `CoroutineContext`: A collection of elements, primarily:\n   - `Job`: Controls coroutine lifecycle, cancellation, and hierarchy.\n   - `CoroutineDispatcher`: Specifies which thread pool executes the code (`Main`, `IO`, `Default`).\n   - `CoroutineExceptionHandler`: Handles uncaught exceptions.\n   - `CoroutineName`: Useful for debugging.\n\nStructured Concurrency Principles:\n1. Hierarchy & Lifetime: Every child coroutine launched within a scope is attached to the parent Job. The parent scope does not complete until all its children have completed.\n2. Cancellation Propagation: If a parent coroutine is cancelled, all child coroutines are recursively cancelled.\n3. Failure Propagation: By default (standard `Job`), if a child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it cancels its parent, which in turn cancels all other siblings.\n4. Leak Prevention: No coroutine can run untracked. In Android, when a `ViewModel` clears (`onCleared()`), `viewModelScope` cancels all running child jobs, terminating network requests and computations automatically.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nKotlin Coroutines provide structured asynchronous programming. Unlike unmanaged threads, coroutines are organized in a strict parent-child hierarchy governed by three pillars:\n1. `CoroutineScope`: Defines the lifecycle boundary of coroutines.\n2. `CoroutineContext`: A collection of elements, primarily:\n   - `Job`: Controls coroutine lifecycle, cancellation, and hierarchy.\n   - `CoroutineDispatcher`: Specifies which thread pool executes the code (`Main`, `IO`, `Default`).\n   - `CoroutineExceptionHandler`: Handles uncaught exceptions.\n   - `CoroutineName`: Useful for debugging.\n\nStructured Concurrency Principles:\n1. Hierarchy & Lifetime: Every child coroutine launched within a scope is attached to the parent Job. The parent scope does not complete until all its children have completed.\n2. Cancellation Propagation: If a parent coroutine is cancelled, all child coroutines are recursively cancelled.\n3. Failure Propagation: By default (standard `Job`), if a child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it cancels its parent, which in turn cancels all other siblings.\n4. Leak Prevention: No coroutine can run untracked. In Android, when a `ViewModel` clears (`onCleared()`), `viewModelScope` cancels all running child jobs, terminating network requests and computations automatically.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\nclass StructuredConcurrencyDemoViewModel : ViewModel() {\n\n    fun executeStructuredTask() {\n        // viewModelScope provides structured concurrency tied to ViewModel lifecycle\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            println(\"Parent started\")\n\n            // Child 1: Simulating background calculation\n            launch(Dispatchers.Default) {\n                while (isActive) { // Respects cancellation cooperatively\n                    // Do work...\n                }\n            }\n\n            // Child 2: Network operation\n            launch(Dispatchers.IO) {\n                // If this throws an exception, Parent is notified and Child 1 is cancelled!\n            }\n\n            // Creating a bounded child scope using coroutineScope\n            coroutineScope {\n                val data1 = async { fetchPart1() }\n                val data2 = async { fetchPart2() }\n                combine(data1.await(), data2.await())\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private suspend fun fetchPart1(): String = \"Data1\"\n    private suspend fun fetchPart2(): String = \"Data2\"\n    private fun combine(a: String, b: String) {}\n}\n```",
