    shortDescription:     "Jetpack Compose is a modern, fully declarative UI toolkit. In Compose, you describe your UI as a series of composable functions (`@Composable`) that convert application state into UI elements. Key Fundamentals:\n1. Recomp",
    explanation:     "Jetpack Compose is a modern, fully declarative UI toolkit. In Compose, you describe your UI as a series of composable functions (`@Composable`) that convert application state into UI elements. Key Fundamentals:\n1. Recomp",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is a modern, fully declarative UI toolkit. In Compose, you describe your UI as a series of composable functions (`@Composable`) that convert application state into UI elements.\n\nKey Fundamentals:\n1. Recomposition:\n   - When observed state changes, Compose re-executes only the composable functions that depend on that specific state, skipping unaffected parts of the UI tree.\n   - Recomposition is intelligent, concurrent, and can be cancelled mid-flight if state changes again before composition finishes.\n2. State Hoisting:\n   - A design pattern where state is moved up to make a composable stateless.\n   - A stateless composable takes state as a parameter (`value: T`) and emits events via lambdas (`onValueChange: (T) -> Unit`). This maximizes reusability, testability, and previews.\n3. Positional Memoization & `remember`:\n   - Composables execute repeatedly. To prevent expensive recalculations or re-instantiations during recomposition, use `remember { ... }`.\n   - To survive configuration changes (rotation), use `rememberSaveable { ... }`.\n4. Layout Phases:\n   - 1. Composition (What to show) -> 2. Layout/Measure/Place (Where to show) -> 3. Drawing (How to render).",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is a modern, fully declarative UI toolkit. In Compose, you describe your UI as a series of composable functions (`@Composable`) that convert application state into UI elements.\n\nKey Fundamentals:\n1. Recomposition:\n   - When observed state changes, Compose re-executes only the composable functions that depend on that specific state, skipping unaffected parts of the UI tree.\n   - Recomposition is intelligent, concurrent, and can be cancelled mid-flight if state changes again before composition finishes.\n2. State Hoisting:\n   - A design pattern where state is moved up to make a composable stateless.\n   - A stateless composable takes state as a parameter (`value: T`) and emits events via lambdas (`onValueChange: (T) -> Unit`). This maximizes reusability, testability, and previews.\n3. Positional Memoization & `remember`:\n   - Composables execute repeatedly. To prevent expensive recalculations or re-instantiations during recomposition, use `remember { ... }`.\n   - To survive configuration changes (rotation), use `rememberSaveable { ... }`.\n4. Layout Phases:\n   - 1. Composition (What to show) -> 2. Layout/Measure/Place (Where to show) -> 3. Drawing (How to render).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// 1. Stateless Composable (State Hoisted) - Highly reusable & testable\n@Composable\nfun CounterWidget(\n    count: Int,\n    onIncrement: () -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier\n) {\n    Card(modifier = modifier.padding(8.dp)) {\n        Row(\n            modifier = Modifier.padding(16.dp),\n            verticalAlignment = Alignment.CenterVertically\n        ) {\n            Text(text = \"Count: $count\", style = MaterialTheme.typography.titleMedium)\n            Spacer(modifier = Modifier.width(16.dp))\n            Button(onClick = onIncrement) {\n                Text(\"+1\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// 2. Stateful Composable holding state using rememberSaveable\n@Composable\nfun CounterContainer() {\n    // Survives screen rotation and process recreation\n    var count by rememberSaveable { mutableIntStateOf(0) }\n\n    CounterWidget(\n        count = count,\n        onIncrement = { count++ }\n    )\n}\n```",
