    shortDescription:     "In Kotlin Coroutines, the choice between a regular `Job` and a `SupervisorJob` completely alters failure propagation in the coroutine hierarchy: 1. Standard `Job`:\n   - Two-way failure propagation (Bi-directional).\n   - ",
    explanation:     "In Kotlin Coroutines, the choice between a regular `Job` and a `SupervisorJob` completely alters failure propagation in the coroutine hierarchy: 1. Standard `Job`:\n   - Two-way failure propagation (Bi-directional).\n   - ",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nIn Kotlin Coroutines, the choice between a regular `Job` and a `SupervisorJob` completely alters failure propagation in the coroutine hierarchy:\n\n1. Standard `Job`:\n   - Two-way failure propagation (Bi-directional).\n   - If one child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it immediately cancels its parent Job.\n   - The parent Job then cancels all other running sibling coroutines.\n   - The entire coroutine tree collapses.\n\n2. `SupervisorJob`:\n   - One-way failure propagation (Uni-directional down only).\n   - If one child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it does NOT cancel its parent `SupervisorJob`.\n   - Consequently, sibling coroutines continue executing undisturbed.\n   - Cancellation from parent to children still works: If the parent `SupervisorJob` itself is cancelled (e.g., when the ViewModel is destroyed), all children are cancelled.\n\nWhy `viewModelScope` uses `SupervisorJob`:\nInside an Android `ViewModel`, multiple independent user operations occur concurrently (e.g., fetching profile data and fetching notification badges). If fetching notification badges fails with a network exception, we do NOT want that to cancel the active profile data download. Therefore, `viewModelScope` is internally configured with `SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.Main.immediate`.\n\nCrucial Exception Handling Gotcha:\nA `SupervisorJob` only isolates failure; it does NOT suppress or swallow exceptions! An uncaught exception in a child `launch` will still reach the thread's default UncaughtExceptionHandler and CRASH the app unless caught with `try-catch` or a `CoroutineExceptionHandler`.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nIn Kotlin Coroutines, the choice between a regular `Job` and a `SupervisorJob` completely alters failure propagation in the coroutine hierarchy:\n\n1. Standard `Job`:\n   - Two-way failure propagation (Bi-directional).\n   - If one child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it immediately cancels its parent Job.\n   - The parent Job then cancels all other running sibling coroutines.\n   - The entire coroutine tree collapses.\n\n2. `SupervisorJob`:\n   - One-way failure propagation (Uni-directional down only).\n   - If one child coroutine fails with an uncaught exception, it does NOT cancel its parent `SupervisorJob`.\n   - Consequently, sibling coroutines continue executing undisturbed.\n   - Cancellation from parent to children still works: If the parent `SupervisorJob` itself is cancelled (e.g., when the ViewModel is destroyed), all children are cancelled.\n\nWhy `viewModelScope` uses `SupervisorJob`:\nInside an Android `ViewModel`, multiple independent user operations occur concurrently (e.g., fetching profile data and fetching notification badges). If fetching notification badges fails with a network exception, we do NOT want that to cancel the active profile data download. Therefore, `viewModelScope` is internally configured with `SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.Main.immediate`.\n\nCrucial Exception Handling Gotcha:\nA `SupervisorJob` only isolates failure; it does NOT suppress or swallow exceptions! An uncaught exception in a child `launch` will still reach the thread's default UncaughtExceptionHandler and CRASH the app unless caught with `try-catch` or a `CoroutineExceptionHandler`.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\nclass SupervisorJobDemoViewModel : ViewModel() {\n\n    // Custom scope demonstration showing SupervisorJob mechanics\n    private val customScope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.IO)\n\n    fun executeIndependentTasks() {\n        // Child 1: Fails with RuntimeException\n        customScope.launch {\n            println(\"Child 1 started\")\n            throw RuntimeException(\"Child 1 failed!\")\n            // Child 1 dies, but does NOT cancel customScope or Child 2\n        }\n\n        // Child 2: Sibling task\n        customScope.launch {\n            println(\"Child 2 started\")\n            delay(1000)\n            println(\"Child 2 completed successfully!\") // WILL execute!\n        }\n    }\n\n    override fun onCleared() {\n        super.onCleared()\n        customScope.cancel() // Cancels all children when ViewModel is destroyed\n    }\n}\n```",
