    shortDescription:     "\"Parameter drilling\" occurs when intermediate composables must accept and pass down properties that only a deeply nested child needs, cluttering function signatures and tightly coupling components. Senior-Level Strategie",
    explanation:     "\"Parameter drilling\" occurs when intermediate composables must accept and pass down properties that only a deeply nested child needs, cluttering function signatures and tightly coupling components. Senior-Level Strategie",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\n\"Parameter drilling\" occurs when intermediate composables must accept and pass down properties that only a deeply nested child needs, cluttering function signatures and tightly coupling components.\n\nSenior-Level Strategies to Avoid Parameter Drilling:\n\n1. Shared ViewModel (Recommended for Feature Screens):\n   - Scope a ViewModel to the parent navigation graph or host Activity using Jetpack Navigation Compose.\n   - Deeply nested child composables retrieve the same ViewModel instance via `hiltViewModel()` or `viewModel(viewModelStoreOwner)`.\n\n2. CompositionLocal (For Ambient, Cross-Cutting Dependencies):\n   - `CompositionLocal` allows data to be passed implicitly down the composition tree without explicit parameters.\n   - Use `staticCompositionLocalOf` (rarely changing data like Themes, Design tokens) or `compositionLocalOf` (changing data).\n   - Standard examples: `LocalContext`, `LocalColorScheme`, `LocalContentColor`.\n   - Caution: Do not abuse for ordinary business state; it hides dependencies and makes previewing difficult.\n\n3. Slot APIs & Component Composition (Clean UI Pattern):\n   - Instead of passing raw data down through multiple containers, use Slot APIs (`content: @Composable () -> Unit`). The parent configures the child directly, eliminating intermediate parameter passing.\n\n4. State Holders / Scoped UI State:\n   - Encapsulate related UI parameters into a dedicated state holder class (`rememberMyScreenState()`).",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\n\"Parameter drilling\" occurs when intermediate composables must accept and pass down properties that only a deeply nested child needs, cluttering function signatures and tightly coupling components.\n\nSenior-Level Strategies to Avoid Parameter Drilling:\n\n1. Shared ViewModel (Recommended for Feature Screens):\n   - Scope a ViewModel to the parent navigation graph or host Activity using Jetpack Navigation Compose.\n   - Deeply nested child composables retrieve the same ViewModel instance via `hiltViewModel()` or `viewModel(viewModelStoreOwner)`.\n\n2. CompositionLocal (For Ambient, Cross-Cutting Dependencies):\n   - `CompositionLocal` allows data to be passed implicitly down the composition tree without explicit parameters.\n   - Use `staticCompositionLocalOf` (rarely changing data like Themes, Design tokens) or `compositionLocalOf` (changing data).\n   - Standard examples: `LocalContext`, `LocalColorScheme`, `LocalContentColor`.\n   - Caution: Do not abuse for ordinary business state; it hides dependencies and makes previewing difficult.\n\n3. Slot APIs & Component Composition (Clean UI Pattern):\n   - Instead of passing raw data down through multiple containers, use Slot APIs (`content: @Composable () -> Unit`). The parent configures the child directly, eliminating intermediate parameter passing.\n\n4. State Holders / Scoped UI State:\n   - Encapsulate related UI parameters into a dedicated state holder class (`rememberMyScreenState()`).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// --- SOLUTION 1: CompositionLocal for Ambient Data ---\ndata class AppUserPermissions(val canEdit: Boolean, val canDelete: Boolean)\n\n// Define CompositionLocal\nval LocalUserPermissions = staticCompositionLocalOf<AppUserPermissions> {\n    error(\"No permissions provided\")\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun MainAppScreen() {\n    val permissions = remember { AppUserPermissions(canEdit = true, canDelete = false) }\n\n    // Provide the value down the tree implicitly\n    CompositionLocalProvider(LocalUserPermissions provides permissions) {\n        DeepContainer()\n    }\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DeepContainer() {\n    // Notice: DeepContainer does NOT need to accept permissions as a parameter!\n    DeepNestedChild()\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DeepNestedChild() {\n    // Consume implicitly\n    val permissions = LocalUserPermissions.current\n\n    if (permissions.canEdit) {\n        Button(onClick = { /* edit */ }) { Text(\"Edit Item\") }\n    }\n}\n\n// --- SOLUTION 2: Slot API Pattern (Decoupled Layouts) ---\n@Composable\nfun PageLayout(\n    header: @Composable () -> Unit,\n    content: @Composable () -> Unit\n) {\n    Column {\n        header()\n        content()\n    }\n}\n```",
