    difficulty:     "HARD",
    interviewType:     "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription:     "Jetpack Compose is built on the strict architectural principle of **Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF)**. In UDF, state flows in only one direction (down), and events flow in the opposite direction (up). The 4-Step Cycle of ",
    explanation:     "Jetpack Compose is built on the strict architectural principle of **Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF)**. In UDF, state flows in only one direction (down), and events flow in the opposite direction (up). The 4-Step Cycle of ",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is built on the strict architectural principle of **Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF)**. In UDF, state flows in only one direction (down), and events flow in the opposite direction (up).\n\nThe 4-Step Cycle of UDF in Compose:\n1. State Flow Down: The State Holder (usually a ViewModel) manages the single source of truth and exposes an immutable UI state (`StateFlow<UiState>`). This state flows downward into composable functions as function arguments.\n2. Rendering: Composables convert the received state into UI elements on the screen.\n3. Event Flow Up: User actions (button clicks, gestures, text input) are captured by the Composable and immediately emitted upward as lambdas/events (`onNameChanged`, `onItemClicked`) to the ViewModel.\n4. State Mutation: The ViewModel processes the event, executes business logic/use cases, and updates the `MutableStateFlow`. This emits a new immutable state, triggering intelligent Recomposition of only the affected composables.\n\nWhy UDF is Essential:\n- Testability: ViewModels can be tested purely with unit tests without UI frameworks.\n- State Consistency: Eliminates synchronization bugs since there is only one source of truth.\n- Decoupling: Composables remain stateless, reusable, and previewable.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nJetpack Compose is built on the strict architectural principle of **Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF)**. In UDF, state flows in only one direction (down), and events flow in the opposite direction (up).\n\nThe 4-Step Cycle of UDF in Compose:\n1. State Flow Down: The State Holder (usually a ViewModel) manages the single source of truth and exposes an immutable UI state (`StateFlow<UiState>`). This state flows downward into composable functions as function arguments.\n2. Rendering: Composables convert the received state into UI elements on the screen.\n3. Event Flow Up: User actions (button clicks, gestures, text input) are captured by the Composable and immediately emitted upward as lambdas/events (`onNameChanged`, `onItemClicked`) to the ViewModel.\n4. State Mutation: The ViewModel processes the event, executes business logic/use cases, and updates the `MutableStateFlow`. This emits a new immutable state, triggering intelligent Recomposition of only the affected composables.\n\nWhy UDF is Essential:\n- Testability: ViewModels can be tested purely with unit tests without UI frameworks.\n- State Consistency: Eliminates synchronization bugs since there is only one source of truth.\n- Decoupling: Composables remain stateless, reusable, and previewable.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// 1. Immutable State\ndata class TaskListUiState(\n    val tasks: List<String> = emptyList(),\n    val newTaskInput: String = \"\"\n)\n\n// 2. ViewModel managing State and handling Events\nclass TaskViewModel : ViewModel() {\n    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(TaskListUiState())\n    val uiState: StateFlow<TaskListUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()\n\n    fun onInputChanged(newText: String) {\n        _uiState.update { it.copy(newTaskInput = newText) }\n    }\n\n    fun onAddTask() {\n        _uiState.update { current ->\n            if (current.newTaskInput.isNotBlank()) {\n                current.copy(\n                    tasks = current.tasks + current.newTaskInput,\n                    newTaskInput = \"\"\n                )\n            } else current\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// 3. UI Layer (State flows DOWN, Events flow UP)\n@Composable\nfun TaskScreen(viewModel: TaskViewModel = viewModel()) {\n    val state by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n\n    TaskContent(\n        state = state,\n        onInputChange = viewModel::onInputChanged, // Event UP\n        onAddTask = viewModel::onAddTask           // Event UP\n    )\n}\n\n// Stateless Composable\n@Composable\nfun TaskContent(\n    state: TaskListUiState,                     // State DOWN\n    onInputChange: (String) -> Unit,           // Event UP\n    onAddTask: () -> Unit                      // Event UP\n) {\n    Column(modifier = Modifier.padding(16.dp)) {\n        OutlinedTextField(\n            value = state.newTaskInput,\n            onValueChange = onInputChange,\n            label = { Text(\"New Task\") }\n        )\n        Button(onClick = onAddTask) {\n            Text(\"Add\")\n        }\n        LazyColumn {\n            items(state.tasks) { task ->\n                Text(text = task, modifier = Modifier.padding(8.dp))\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
