    shortDescription:     "In a Senior interview, resolving a state bug requires demonstrating an understanding of Compose\u2019s snapshot state system and recomposition mechanics: Common Advanced Compose State Bugs:\n1. Recomposition Loops: Writing to ",
    explanation:     "In a Senior interview, resolving a state bug requires demonstrating an understanding of Compose\u2019s snapshot state system and recomposition mechanics: Common Advanced Compose State Bugs:\n1. Recomposition Loops: Writing to ",
    sampleAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nIn a Senior interview, resolving a state bug requires demonstrating an understanding of Compose\u2019s snapshot state system and recomposition mechanics:\n\nCommon Advanced Compose State Bugs:\n1. Recomposition Loops: Writing to a state inside a Composable without wrapping it in an effect, triggering an infinite recomposition loop.\n2. Lost Updates / Stale Closures: Capturing an unstable variable inside a long-running coroutine or lambda without updating the reference.\n3. Unstable Class Recompositions: Compose re-evaluating composables that should have been skipped because a parameter class is inferred as `unstable` (e.g., standard `List<T>` from `java.util`).\n\nResolution Protocol:\n1. Inspect Layout Inspector: Use Android Studio Layout Inspector with Recomposition Counters to find which composable is recomposing continuously.\n2. Run Compose Compiler Metrics: Generate stability reports (`-Pmyapp.enableComposeCompilerReports=true`) to inspect which UI classes are marked as unstable.\n3. Mark Classes Stable: Wrap domain models with `@Immutable` or `@Stable`, or use KotlinX Immutable Collections (`ImmutableList<T>`).\n4. Enforce MVI / Unidirectional State: Ensure ViewModel exposes a single, atomically updated data class.",
    detailedAnswer:     "**Answer:**\n\nIn a Senior interview, resolving a state bug requires demonstrating an understanding of Compose\u2019s snapshot state system and recomposition mechanics:\n\nCommon Advanced Compose State Bugs:\n1. Recomposition Loops: Writing to a state inside a Composable without wrapping it in an effect, triggering an infinite recomposition loop.\n2. Lost Updates / Stale Closures: Capturing an unstable variable inside a long-running coroutine or lambda without updating the reference.\n3. Unstable Class Recompositions: Compose re-evaluating composables that should have been skipped because a parameter class is inferred as `unstable` (e.g., standard `List<T>` from `java.util`).\n\nResolution Protocol:\n1. Inspect Layout Inspector: Use Android Studio Layout Inspector with Recomposition Counters to find which composable is recomposing continuously.\n2. Run Compose Compiler Metrics: Generate stability reports (`-Pmyapp.enableComposeCompilerReports=true`) to inspect which UI classes are marked as unstable.\n3. Mark Classes Stable: Wrap domain models with `@Immutable` or `@Stable`, or use KotlinX Immutable Collections (`ImmutableList<T>`).\n4. Enforce MVI / Unidirectional State: Ensure ViewModel exposes a single, atomically updated data class.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```kotlin\n// Problem: Standard List causes Compose to re-run recomposition on every parent recompose\n// because standard Kotlin List is an interface that cannot guarantee immutability to the compiler.\n\n// 1. Mark State Immutable or use ImmutableList\n@Immutable\ndata class OrderHistoryUiState(\n    val orders: ImmutableList<OrderModel> = persistentListOf(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false\n)\n\n@HiltViewModel\nclass OrderHistoryViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: OrderRepository\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(OrderHistoryUiState(isLoading = true))\n    val uiState: StateFlow<OrderHistoryUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()\n\n    init {\n        loadOrders()\n    }\n\n    private fun loadOrders() {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            repository.getOrders()\n                .collect { orderList ->\n                    // Atomic update using immutable collections\n                    _uiState.update {\n                        it.copy(\n                            orders = orderList.toImmutableList(),\n                            isLoading = false\n                        )\n                    }\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// 2. Composable that properly skips recomposition when orders have not changed\n@Composable\nfun OrderHistoryList(\n    state: OrderHistoryUiState,\n    onOrderClick: (String) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier\n) {\n    LazyColumn(modifier = modifier) {\n        items(\n            items = state.orders,\n            key = { it.id } // ALWAYS supply key for lazy lists to prevent re-drawing\n        ) { order ->\n            OrderItemRow(order = order, onClick = onOrderClick)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
